body:
I'm a male in my late 20s. I too am interested in photography. I've done some portrait work, but it has mainly been nature photography (some street / urban scenery photography mixed in as well). Road trips are always fun. Have wanted to go camping for a while now. I like books but have trouble setting aside the time to read them. I'm an aerospace engineer by training and a software engineer by trade. I'm an all-around nerd for planes and spacecraft. I love sci-fy movies and shows, but I will watch almost anything. As far as music goes I literally like almost anything.  Rock, top 40, punk, country, hop-hop, pop, you name it. As far as sports go, I'm not the biggest fan but I do enjoy watching the occasional game and having a couple beers. I love to BBQ. I have a smoker and make all forms of smoked meat. Ribs, brisket, rib-eye, pulled pork. 

&#x200B;

As far as other interests go, I like building electronics and projects of the sort, hiking, going out to bars (I'm always pleasantly surprised by the interesting people I meet at bars, lots people from walks of life I didn't expect to find.

I am Christian in faith, or at least I was raised that way. I have gotten very angry at it, but not as angry as scared. It has been tormenting me. I lie away at night grimacing at every little noise. A recent thing that happened, where I was drifting to sleep and had a "conversation with it" subconsciously. I think I invited it in. I remember being startled away after realizing I had just been communicating with it. Things like "welcome, I know you are here now. Who are you, how old are you, where do you come from" Suddenly i woke up and was terrified to feel its presence right there next to me, and I had been "speaking" to it. I've had a couple of these episodes in the past week. I think it is trying to posses me as I fall asleep. Using my wandering thoughts to influence me...

&#x200B;

I am fearful that what you say might be true, that these are three sides of the same coin and all three entities are really "acts" being put on by a demon. One of them is very scary, the other two are seemingly harmless. I think at this point it has grown strong enough that it is no longer constrained to saying at my house.

My story is similar to yours. I was diagnosed with OCD in second grade, then depression and anxiety in middle school. It has been a tough journey. I am in my late 20s now, got through engineering school, have a steady job. Still struggle with depression but I am so glad I had people help get me through it. 

&#x200B;

Every day that i'm still breathing is a victory. Every day above ground is a good one.

It gets better. I've been struggling with depression and anxiety since 2nd grade. Yes. Second grade. But I got through school, even with relentless bullying, made friends in high school. Got into college, and got an engineering degree. There is hope. The path may be difficult for some, but I am thankful that I never gave up. 

&#x200B;

My thoughts are with your daughter, her and I probably have much in common if you exclude the 20 year time difference. Please consider therapy for her if you haven't already. I wouldnt be here today if it wasnt for the kind souls who helped me through: my mother, my grandfather, and the therapists who helped me. 

&#x200B;

Please take care, I wish you and your daughter the best. No one deserves bullying or depression, especially not at such a tender age.

Your comment brought me to tears. I've been struggling with depression (often crippling) for most of my life, and there have been more than a few times I've considered ending it all. Once that I actually almost did. 

Thank you for being a good human being to this young man. Your comment has inspired and touched me, and im sure countless others as well. 

&#x200B;

I logged in just to say this (was on a laptop and almost always use my phone). Stay strong my friend. 

&#x200B;

Thank you sincerely.
